in\nL168: \nL169: 【86†Branches】【87†Tags】\nL170: \nL171: [Input: Go to file]\nL172: \nL173: Go to file\nL174: \nL175: Code\nL176: \nL177: Open more actions menu\nL178: \nL179: ## Latest commit\nL180: \nL181: \nL182: \nL183: ## History\nL184: \nL185: 1 Commit\nL186: \nL187: ## Folders and files\nL188: \nL189: Name  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\nL190: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL191: 【88†benchmarks】  | 【88†benchmarks】  |    |\nL192: 【89†.gitignore】  | 【89†.gitignore】  |    |\nL193: 【90†LICENSE】  | 【90†LICENSE】  |    |\nL194: 【91†README.md】  | 【91†README.md】  |    |\nL195: 【92†REPORT.md】  | 【92†REPORT.md】  |    |\nL196: ## Repository files navigation\nL197: \nL198:   *   * 【93†README】\nL199:   * 【93†MIT license】\nL200: \nL201: More items\nL202: # ICICLE NTT on consumer Blackwell (RTX 5070, sm_120)\nL203: \nL204: An independent, single-GPU empirical characterization of 【94†ICICLE】 v4.0.0's NTT on a consumer NVIDIA Blackwell GPU — where time actually goes, end to end and per kernel — plus a prototype that quantifies one recoverable bottleneck.\nL205: Shared in case it is useful to the ICICLE community and others bringing GPU ZK stacks to consumer Blackwell. Methodology is empirical before theoretical: every claim is a measurement, and the negative results are reported with the same weight as the positive one.\nL206: \nL207: > Full write-up with all profiles and interpretation: 【92†REPORT.md】.\nL208: ## Findings (TL;DR)\nL209:   1. ICICLE runs on Blackwell only through PTX-JIT. The shipped v4.0.0 CUDA backend embeds SASS up to sm_89 (Ada) plus `compute_89` PTX, but no sm_120 cubin; the driver JIT-compiles the PTX to sm_120 at first launch. This is more robust than most of the ecosystem (which ships no PTX and hard-fails on Blackwell), but it carries first-launch JIT latency and a PTX-forward-compatibility dependency.\nL210:   2. No JIT throughput penalty on field arithmetic. A BabyBear Montgomery-modmul kernel built native sm_120 vs `compute_89`-PTX-then-JIT measured within run-to-run noise (1213 vs 1257 Gmodmul/s). A native build's value is removing JIT latency and the PTX dependency, not speeding up the arithmetic.\nL211:   3. Butterflies are already at the memory ceiling; the digit-reversal pass is the bottleneck. At 2²², `ntt64` runs at 84.7% and `ntt32dit` at 76.5% of DRAM peak. The single largest kernel, `reorder_digits_and_normalize` (39% of NTT kernel time), runs at only 46% of peak with a global-store efficiency of 4 of 32 bytes per sector (12.5%) — the textbook scatter signature of digit reversal.\nL212:   4. That headroom is recoverable (prototype). On the same GPU/size/dtype, a scattered bit-reversal reproducing ICICLE's exact 4/32 store signature runs at ~375 GB/s, while a shared-memory-staged coalesced permutation runs at ~780 GB/s (~2×). Since the reorder is 39% of NTT kernel time, a coalesced (COBRA-style) digit-reversal would translate to an estimated ~15–20% end-to-end NTT speedup.\nL213: ## Results at a glance\nL214: Measurement (RTX 5070, 2²² BabyBear)  | Result\nL215: --- | ---\nL216: modmul — native sm_120 vs JIT compute_89  | 1212.8 vs 1256.9 Gmodmul/s (within noise)\nL217: `ntt64` DRAM throughput  | 84.7% of peak\nL218: `ntt32dit` DRAM throughput  | 76.5% of peak\nL219: `reorder_digits_and_normalize` — share / DRAM / store eff.  | 39% of NTT time / 46.2% of peak / 4-of-32 bytes/sector\nL220: coalesced copy (bandwidth ceiling)  | ~1320 GB/s\nL221: scattered bit-reversal (= ICICLE reorder pattern)  | ~375 GB/s\nL222: shared-memory tiled transpose (coalesced permutation)  | ~780 GB/s (~2.1×)\nL223: single-shot NTT over host data (2²²)  | ~74% PCIe, ~12% compute\nL224: ## Layout\nL225: \nL226:     `benchmarks/\nL227:       bench_modmul.cu     # Finding 2: native sm_120 vs JIT compute_89 modmul throughput\nL228:       ntt_bench.cpp       # Findings 1 & 3: ICICLE NTT harness (links the v4 babybear frontend)\nL229:       bitrev_bench.cu     # Finding 4: recoverable-bandwidth prototype (copy / scatter / transpose)\nL230:     REPORT.md             # full characterization, profiles, and interpretation\nL231:     `\nL232: ## Quickstart\nL233: \nL234:     # Finding 2 — field-arithmetic JIT vs native (no ICICLE needed)\nL235:     nvcc -O3 -arch=sm_120                                  benchmarks/bench_modmul.cu -o bench_native\nL236:     nvcc -O3 -gencode arch=compute_89,code=compute_89      benchmarks/bench_modmul.cu -o bench_jit\nL237:     ./bench_native 2048 ; ./bench_jit 2048\nL238: \nL239:     # Finding 4 — recoverable-bandwidth prototype (no ICICLE needed)\nL240:     nvcc -O3 -arch=sm_120 benchmarks/bitrev_bench.cu -o bitrev_bench\nL241:     ./bitrev_bench\nL242:     sudo -E $(which ncu) --kernel-name \"regex:k_copy|k_bitrev_scatter|k_transpose_tiled\" \\\nL243:          -c 3 --section MemoryWorkloadAnalysis ./bitrev_bench\nL244: \nL245:     # Findings 1 & 3 — ICICLE NTT harness (needs ICICLE v4 babybear frontend + CUDA backend)\nL246:     #   see header of benchmarks/ntt_bench.cpp for the exact build/link line\nL247:     export ICICLE_BACKEND_INSTALL_DIR=$HOME/ingo/icicle-cuda-backend/icicle/lib/backend\nL248:     ./ntt_bench 24                                   # sweep 2^16..2^24\nL249:     nsys profile --stats=true ./ntt_bench 22 22      # kernel + memcpy breakdown\nL250:     sudo -E $(which ncu) --kernel-name \"regex:ntt32dit|ntt64|reorder_digits\" -c 6 --set basic ./ntt_bench 22 22\nL251: Each harness is a single, correctness-gated file; build/run/profile commands are in its header comment.\nL252: ## Environment\nL253: \nL254: RTX 5070 (GB205, sm_120, 48 SMs, ~672 GB/s GDDR7, ~48 MB L2) · driver 575.57.08 / CUDA 12.9 · Ryzen 7 9800X3D, 128 GB · Ubuntu 24.04 (kernel 6.8) · ICICLE v4.0.0 (frontend from source, `FIELD=babybear`; CUDA backend `ubuntu22-cuda122`). All numbers are from this single machine.\nL255: \nL256: ## License\nL257: \nL258: MIT — see 【90†LICENSE】. The harnesses are original; they call ICICLE through its public API and do not redistribute it.\nL259: ## About\nL260: \nL261: Empirical characterization of ICICLE NTT on consumer NVIDIA Blackwell (RTX 5070, sm_120), with a prototype for the digit-reversal bottleneck.\nL262: \nL263: ### Topics\nL264: \nL265: 【71†cryptography】【72†cuda】【73†gpu】【74†gpu-computing】【75†icicle】【76†ntt】【77†nvidia-blackwell】【78†performance】【79†profiling】【80†zero-knowledge】\nL266: \nL267: ### Resources\nL268: \nL269: 【81†Readme】\nL270: \nL271: 【82†MIT license】\nL272: \nL273: 【83†Activity】\nL274: \nL275: ### Stars\nL276: \nL277: 0 stars\nL278: \nL279: ### Watchers\nL280: \nL281: 0 watching\nL282: \nL283: ### Forks\nL284: \nL285: 【84†0 forks】\nL286: \nL287: 【85†Report repository】\nL288: \nL289: ## Releases\nL290: \nL291: ## Packages\nL292: \nL293: ## Contributors\nL294: ## Languages\nL295: \nL296: You can’t perform that action at this time.\n"
